    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my realtor just mentioned something about needing an escrow account for this house i'm looking at in toronto. i've never bought a place before so i'm kinda lost. can u explain what escrow actually is and why it's even used in the closing process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in the context of real estate, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a way to ensure that the funds are only released when the agreed-upon terms are fulfilled. Think of it like a temporary stash of coins, guarded by a trusty (and not too mischievous) group of accountants, who will only hand over the coins when the seller has vacated the premises and the buyer has taken possession of the property.\n\nIn the case of a home purchase, the escrow account typically holds funds for property taxes and insurance, which are usually paid annually. The escrow account acts as a buffer, so that when the time comes to pay these expenses, the funds are already set aside, and the buyer doesn't have to worry about scrambling to come up with the cash. It's a bit like having a team of diligent goblins, working behind the scenes to ensure that all the necessary expenses are taken care of, without the buyer having to lift a finger.\n\nNow, in Toronto, the escrow account is usually managed by the lender, and it's a standard practice in the city. The realtor's mention of it is just a heads up that this is a requirement for",
