      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No worries, I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. To answer your question, the escrow account is typically managed by a third-party escrow company, which is usually hired by the lender. These companies are regulated by government agencies, such as the Ontario Securities Commission, to ensure that they operate in a fair and transparent manner.\n\nThink of the escrow company as a neutral, impartial group of accountants, who are tasked with holding the funds until the conditions are met. They're like a team of diligent, if slightly grumpy, goblins, who are only interested in doing their job to the letter.\n\nIn terms of safety, the escrow company is usually bonded and insured, which means that if they were to misbehave, the funds would be protected. The lender also typically has a say in the escrow company's selection process, so they can ensure that the company is reputable and trustworthy.\n\nAs for how you know the money is safe, you can usually find information about the escrow company and their policies on the lender's website or by asking the realtor. It's also a good idea to review the escrow agreement carefully, to make sure you understand how the funds will be managed and when they",
